{
  "requirement": "Every call constructs a fresh `ValidationContext` identifying the source node\nand calls `Criterion::execute` with that context. Its completed answer, including\na negative assessment, and captured dependencies form the returned `Fingerprinted`\nunder {cite}`requirements.sdk.graph.evidence.inputs`.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "I judged this from the supplied test source only. I did not request implementation source. The requirement `requirements.sdk.graph.running-criteria.validate_node` has three obligations, and the marked tests cover each one.\n\n1. **Fresh `ValidationContext` per call, identifying the source node.**\n   - `concurrent_single_node_validations_keep_separate_traces` in `validation_tests.rs` runs three `verify` calls concurrently on nodes 1–3. Each criterion reads content and edges in its callback. After storing, each record holds only that node's own `Dependency::Node` and `OutgoingEdges` entries. A shared or reused context would mix these traces, so the assertions would catch it.\n   - `historical_freshness_reads_do_not_enter_the_replacement_trace` runs `check_inputs`, which reads node 2, before `verify`. The replacement record then holds only the node 1 read, and the recorded reads are `[2, 1]`. So the new call starts with an empty trace.\n   - `agent_executions_isolate_conversations_and_dependency_traces` runs two concurrent agent executions on the same node. Each gets its own conversation and its own dependency record: alpha stores a node 1 dependency, and beta stores edge and node 2 dependencies.\n   - `EdgeReview` in `agent_edge_tools_record_selections_without_validating_targets` branches on `ctx.node_id()` equalling node 1. Its recorded dependencies and the edge tool results confirm the context identified the source node.\n\n2. **`Criterion::execute` is called with that context.** Every `verify` test runs a criterion whose `execute` uses the context passed to it, through `ctx.ask`, reads and edge lookups. `criteria_keep_typed_answers_and_persist_only_when_requested` in `contracts.rs` asserts that `executions` increments. It also shows that `verify` ignores saved records: a replacement `verify` re-executes and leaves the stored record unchanged.\n\n3. **The completed answer, including a negative assessment, and the captured dependencies form the returned `Fingerprinted`.**\n   - `caller_reuses_negative_answers_and_compares_revisions_explicitly` asserts `!value.output().result` for a negative assessment. The dependencies are empty, and nothing is written before an explicit store.\n   - `criteria_keep_typed_answers_and_persist_only_when_requested` asserts `output()`, `source_node_id()`, `criterion_path()`, `version()` and empty `dependencies()` on a negative answer.\n   - `changing_one_criteria_input_does_not_invalidate_another` and `input_checking_and_execution_keep_separate_observations` assert that the returned dependencies are exactly the reads the criterion performed.\n   - The concurrent and agent tests assert non-empty dependency sets that match the observed reads and edges.\n\nThe cited `requirements.sdk.graph.evidence.inputs` (the `Fingerprinted` accessors) is exercised by these same assertions on `output`, `into_output`, `source_node_id`, `criterion_path`, `version` and `dependencies`. The requirement's text names no error or boundary case beyond the negative answer, and that case is tested. I found no gap.",
